= StakeShuffle Mixing <ch:mixing>

#note[
  *Status: not consensus.* Nothing in this chapter affects the validity of a
  block or transaction. A completed mix is an ordinary regular-tree
  transaction validated by the rules of #chref("transactions"). StakeShuffle
  is nevertheless an _interoperability_ protocol: a client that deviates from
  any byte layout, hash preimage, ordering or key-derivation step described
  here cannot complete a mix with dcrd and dcrwallet peers, and may be
  blamed and excluded as a disruptor. The chapter therefore uses #MUST and
  #SHOULD in their RFC 2119 sense for interoperability, and labels
  relay-node behavior as _policy_. No rule boxes appear in it.
]

StakeShuffle is Decred's peer-to-peer coin-mixing protocol. Wallets that wish
to break the on-chain link between their coins and their future spending
cooperate to build a single _CoinJoin_ transaction in which every
participant contributes inputs and receives one or more outputs of an
identical, agreed value. The protocol makes sure that no participant, relay
node or outside observer learns which participant owns which of the
equal-valued outputs. It is an implementation of CoinShuffle++
@coinshufflepp, more precisely of DiceMix Light @dicemixlight, a simpler
protocol inspired by the DiceMix of that paper, in which a separate
slot-reservation phase precedes an XOR DC-net. It is carried as eight
new message types over the ordinary Decred peer-to-peer network
(#chref("p2p")), with no coordinating server. Full nodes relay the messages
after checking them against a _mixpool_ policy that resists denial of
service.

The reference implementation is the `mixing` module of dcrd: the packages
`mixing` (cryptographic primitives), `mixing/mixclient` (the participant
state machine used by dcrwallet @dcrwallet), `mixing/mixpool` (the message
pool used by both relays and wallets) and `mixing/utxoproof`, together with
the message encodings in `wire/msgmix*.go`.

== Overview <sec:mixing-overview>

=== What is mixed <sec:mixing-what>

Each participant, called a _peer_ in this chapter, contributes one or more
unspent outputs it controls, asks for $m >= 1$ fresh outputs of the common
_mix amount_ (its _message count_), and may ask for one change output. The
final transaction contains every peer's inputs, every peer's change output,
and $sum m$ outputs of exactly the mix amount, each paying a
pay-to-pubkey-hash (P2PKH) script to a key freshly generated by its owner.
Only the mixed outputs are anonymized: inputs and change are publicly linked
to the peer that contributed them. Amounts are public; equality of the mixed
outputs is what makes them indistinguishable.

dcrwallet uses mixing in two ways (informative): to mix ordinary funds into a
"mixed" account in standard denominations, and in its ticket buyer, which
mixes the outputs of the _split transaction_ that funds ticket purchases
(#chref("staking")) so that a ticket cannot be traced to the coins that paid
for it. Both produce the same kind of CoinJoin; the denominations a wallet
chooses are wallet policy and are not specified by dcrd, but peers can only
mix together when their mix amounts are exactly equal
(@sec:mixing-pairing).

=== Threat model and guarantees <sec:mixing-threat>

The guarantees are those of CoinShuffle++ @coinshufflepp:

- *Unlinkability.* An adversary who controls the network and all but two of
  the peers of a successful run cannot tell which honest peer owns which
  honest mixed output with probability better than guessing. The anonymity
  set of an output is the set of honest peers in the final run. The
  adversary does learn the full set of inputs, change outputs and mixed
  outputs.
- *Termination.* A malicious peer can force a run to fail, but every
  failure either identifies the misbehaving peers to all honest peers,
  publicly and verifiably, or is attributable to a timeout. The honest peers
  then rerun without them. The papers assume an honest bulletin board or
  reliable broadcast channel and at least two honest peers for termination;
  dcrd floods messages
  through the peer-to-peer network and gives up below #const("MinPeers")
  peers. With $f$ disruptors, at most about $f + 1$ runs
  are needed, since every failed run excludes at least one of them; DiceMix
  Light states its cost as $4 + 3 f$ communication rounds @dicemixlight.
- *No custody.* Each peer signs only the final transaction, after checking
  that its own outputs are present. Nothing can be stolen.

Two departures from those designs strengthen it. Pairwise keys combine
elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman with the post-quantum key-encapsulation
mechanism Streamlined NTRU Prime 4591#super[761] @ntruprime, so that a
future adversary able to break secp256k1 from a recorded transcript still
faces the lattice scheme ("If sntrup4591761 is discovered to be broken in
the future, the security only reduces to that of ECDH", in the words of the
source). And
every peer commits, in its first session message, to all secrets it will
ever need to reveal, so that blame assignment cannot be gamed by choosing
secrets after the fact. DiceMix Light commits only to the messages to be
mixed, through an additional DC-net over a group sent with the slot
reservation; its other secrets follow deterministically from the key-exchange
secret.

The protocol does not hide network-level metadata. Messages are flooded
through the peer-to-peer network, so a node directly connected to a
participant may infer that a message originated there; random delays
("jitter") on every message reduce, but do not remove, timing correlation.
Reusing an address, or spending change together with mixed outputs, undoes
the mix; that is wallet policy.

=== Protocol at a glance <sec:mixing-glance>

Time is divided into _epochs_ of fixed length (ten minutes on the main
network). Between epoch boundaries, peers broadcast _pair requests_ (PR)
describing what they want to mix and proving that they own the inputs. At
each epoch boundary every waiting peer independently collects the compatible
pair requests it knows of, derives a _session identifier_ from them, and
runs the protocol of @tab:mixing-flow. A _run_ is one attempt; if a run
fails, the survivors start a new run, which in this implementation is always
a new session with its own identifier.

#figure(
  caption: [Messages of a StakeShuffle session, in protocol order],
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, 1fr),
    table.header([Abbr.], [Command], [Sent when], [Purpose]),
    [PR], [`mixpairreq`], [before epoch], [Describe the requested mix, list inputs with ownership proofs; no session yet.],
    [KE], [`mixkeyxchg`], [run start], [Fix the session (PR list and order), publish ECDH and KEM public keys and a commitment to all secrets.],
    [CT], [`mixcphrtxt`], [after all KEs], [One KEM ciphertext to every peer (including self).],
    [SR], [`mixslotres`], [after all CTs], [Slot reservation: padded power sums over the field $FF_F$.],
    [FP], [`mixfactpoly`], [with DC], [Roots of the slot-reservation polynomial, published by peers able to factor it.],
    [DC], [`mixdcnet`], [after roots], [XOR DC-net vectors carrying the 20-byte output hashes in their reserved slots.],
    [CM], [`mixconfirm`], [after DC], [The CoinJoin with the sender's input signatures.],
    [RS], [`mixsecrets`], [on failure], [Reveal the committed secrets for blame assignment.],
  ),
) <tab:mixing-flow>

== Primitives and Notation <sec:mixing-primitives>

The following primitives are used. Byte strings, `||`, `le32`, `be64` and
friends follow #chref("conventions")\; `varint`, `varbytes` and the
transaction serialization follow #chref("encoding").

/ BLAKE-256: The 32-byte hash of #chref("crypto"), written $H(dot)$. Every
  hash in this chapter is a single BLAKE-256, never the double hash.
/ HASH160: RIPEMD-160 of BLAKE-256, as defined in #chref("crypto").
/ secp256k1 and EC-Schnorr-DCRv0: The curve and the 64-byte Schnorr
  signature scheme of #chref("crypto"). Public keys in mixing messages are
  33-byte compressed SEC encodings.
/ Field: All slot-reservation arithmetic is in the prime field $FF_F$,
  where
  $ F = 2^127 - 1 = 170141183460469231731687303715884105727 $
  (#const("F"), hexadecimal `7fff...ff`, 16 bytes). "$x mod F$" always
  denotes the non-negative residue. Field elements are serialized as
  _minimal big-endian_ byte strings (no leading zero bytes; zero is the empty
  string) inside `varbytes`, at most #const("MaxMixFieldValLen") $= 32$ bytes.
  A received value is parsed as an unsigned big-endian integer regardless of
  leading zeros.
/ ChaCha20 PRNG: $"PRNG"(k, n)$ is the keystream of the IETF ChaCha20 stream
  cipher @chacha in the 96-bit-nonce, 32-bit-counter variant of RFC 8439
  @rfc8439,
  with 32-byte key $k$, 12-byte nonce `le32(n) || 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00`
  and initial block counter 0. Reading $ell$ bytes returns the next $ell$
  keystream bytes; reads are consecutive.
  #src("mixing/internal/chacha20prng/prng.go", "New")
/ sntrup4591761: The key-encapsulation mechanism of @sec:mixing-kem.

=== The sntrup4591761 KEM <sec:mixing-kem>

The KEM is _Streamlined NTRU Prime 4591#super[761]_ as specified in the NTRU
Prime paper @ntruprime, in the parameters of its August 2017 revision. dcrd uses a Go port of the reference C
code of that paper, the package

#align(center, raw("github.com/companyzero/sntrup4591761  v0.0.0-20220309191932-9e0f3af2f07a"))

and an implementation #MUST be bit-compatible with it. Its parameters are
$p = 761$, $q = 4591$ and
$t = 143$ (the scheme's own names; this $p$ is unrelated to the peer $p$ of
later sections), so a _t-small_ polynomial has exactly $2t = 286$ non-zero
coefficients in ${-1, 0, 1}$. Sizes are fixed:

#figure(
  caption: [sntrup4591761 object sizes],
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
    table.header([Object], [Bytes], [Content]),
    [Public key], [1218], [Encoding of $h = g \/ (3f)$ in $cal(R)\/q$.],
    [Private key], [1600], [`Encode(f)` (191) `||` `Encode(1/g mod 3)` (191) `||` public key (1218).],
    [Ciphertext], [1047], [First 32 bytes of $"SHA-512"("Encode"(r))$ (confirmation) `||` rounded encoding of $h r$ (1015).],
    [Shared key], [32], [Last 32 bytes of $"SHA-512"("Encode"(r))$.],
  ),
) <tab:mixing-kem>

Decapsulation reports failure unless the recovered $r$ has weight 286, its
re-encryption equals the ciphertext and the confirmation matches.

#pitfall[
  This is the 2017 "sntrup4591761" scheme, *not* the later NIST-submission
  "sntrup761" (whose keys and ciphertexts have different sizes and hashing).
  Implementations from current cryptographic libraries are not compatible.
]

Because blame assignment (@sec:mixing-blame) recomputes another peer's keys
and ciphertexts from its revealed seed, the _consumption of randomness_ must
be reproduced exactly, not only the scheme:

- Each random 32-bit word is 4 bytes read from the source, interpreted as a
  little-endian `int32`.
- _Key generation_: repeat { read 761 words $r_i$ and set
  $g_i = (((r_i "AND" "0x3fffffff") times 3) >> 30) - 1$ } until $g$ is
  invertible modulo 3; then sample $f$ as a t-small element (below).
- _Encapsulation_: sample $r$ as a t-small element.
- _t-small sampling_: read 761 words $w_i$; for $i < 286$ set
  $w_i <- w_i "AND" (-2)$, otherwise $w_i <- (w_i "AND" (-3)) "OR" 1$; reorder
  $w$ with the library's fixed sorting network (@alg:mixing-tsmall-sort, a
  port of the reference implementation's); set coefficient $i$ to
  $(w_i "AND" 3) - 1$.

#algorithm("sntrup4591761 sorting network", src: "github.com/companyzero/sntrup4591761/zx/random.go · sort, minmax")[
  ```python
  def sort761(w, le):              # w: 761 int32 words; le: see pitfall
      n, top = 761, 1
      while top < n - top:
          top += top               # top = 512
      def minmax(i, j):            # exchange so that the "smaller" word is at i
          if le(w[j], w[i]):
              w[i], w[j] = w[j], w[i]
      p = top
      while p > 0:
          for i in range(n - p):
              if i & p == 0: minmax(i, i + p)
          q = top
          while q > p:
              for i in range(n - q):
                  if i & p == 0: minmax(i + p, i + q)
              q >>= 1
          p >>= 1
  ```
] <alg:mixing-tsmall-sort>

#pitfall[
  `minmax` evaluates Go's
  `crypto/subtle.ConstantTimeLessOrEq(int(uint32(a)), int(uint32(b)))`,
  whose result for words of $2^31$ or more depends on the Go release and the
  target's `int` width:

  - Go 1.25 and earlier: `le(a, b)` is the sign bit of the wrapping 32-bit
    difference `int32(a) - int32(b) - 1`. This is not an order relation, and
    the result depends on the exact network above.
  - Go 1.26 and later, 64-bit targets: `le(a, b)` is
    `uint32(a) <= uint32(b)`, so the network sorts the words into ascending
    unsigned order (any correct sort gives the same result).
  - Go 1.26 and later, 32-bit targets: `int(uint32(a))` wraps, `le(a, b)` is
    `int32(a) <= int32(b)`, and the words end up in ascending signed order.

  The same seed therefore yields different sntrup4591761 public keys and
  ciphertexts in the three cases. This was observed with Go 1.24.13, 1.25.0
  and 1.25.14 on amd64 (1.24.13 and 1.25.14 also on 386), Go 1.26.0, 1.26.11
  and 1.27.4 on amd64, and Go 1.26.0, 1.26.11 and 1.27.4 on 386
  (@sec:mixing-vectors); for 1000 random
  inputs the library's t-small output matched @alg:mixing-tsmall-sort with
  the comparison listed for each case every time. Normal mixing is
  unaffected, since any valid key pair works. In blame assignment, however,
  each peer recomputes the others' public keys from their revealed seeds
  with its own build (@sec:mixing-blame-proc, step 1); when the two builds
  fall in different cases above, the recomputed key differs from the KE's
  and the verifier blames a peer that followed the protocol. A new
  implementation #SHOULD use the unsigned comparison (ascending unsigned
  sort) of current 64-bit builds.
]

== Identities, Hashes and Signatures <sec:mixing-signing>

=== Ephemeral identities <sec:mixing-identity>

Every mixing message carries the sender's _identity_: a 33-byte compressed
secp256k1 public key generated freshly for each pair request and discarded
after the mix. A peer #MUST use a new identity for every pair request; the
mixpool rejects a pair request from an identity that has already sent any
message (@sec:mixing-mixpool). All later messages of the peer in every
session formed from that pair request are signed by the same identity.

=== Message hash <sec:mixing-msghash>

Each mixing message is identified by its _message hash_, the BLAKE-256 of its
canonical serialization (the full payload of @sec:mixing-wire, signature
first). Message hashes are the inventory identifiers used for relay and the
references in "seen" lists. They are always used in internal byte order, not
reversed for display.

=== Signatures <sec:mixing-sig>

Every message, including the pair request, is signed with EC-Schnorr-DCRv0
by the identity key. The signed digest binds the message contents, its type,
its session and its run, using an ASCII domain-separation string.

#algorithm("Mixing message signature digest", src: ("mixing/signatures.go · sign, verify, schnorrHash", "wire/msgmix*.go · WriteSignedData"))[
  ```python
  def signed_data_hash(msg) -> bytes:
      # varstring of the command with "-sig" appended (e.g. "mixkeyxchg-sig"),
      # then every field of the message except the leading 64-byte signature,
      # serialized exactly as on the wire
      label = (msg.command + "-sig").encode("ascii")
      return BLAKE256(varint(len(label)) + label + serialize_without_signature(msg))

  def signature_digest(msg) -> bytes:
      sid, run = msg.session_id, msg.run
      if msg.command == "mixpairreq":          # has no session
          sid, run = bytes(32), 0
      s = (b"decred-mix-signature" + b"," + msg.command.encode("ascii") + b"," +
           hex_lower(sid) + b"," + str(run).encode("ascii") + b"," +
           hex_lower(signed_data_hash(msg)))
      return BLAKE256(s)

  def sign(msg, priv):
      msg.signature = ec_schnorr_dcrv0_sign(priv, signature_digest(msg))   # 64 bytes

  def verify(msg) -> bool:
      return (len(msg.identity) == 33 and parse_pubkey(msg.identity) is not None
              and parse_schnorr_sig(msg.signature) is not None
              and ec_schnorr_dcrv0_verify(msg.identity, signature_digest(msg),
                                          msg.signature))
  ```
] <alg:mixing-sig>

`hex_lower` is lowercase hexadecimal of the raw bytes (64 characters for
32 bytes), and the run is written in decimal without leading zeros. Because
the session identifier and run are inside the digest, a signature cannot be
replayed into another session even if the rest of a message were identical.

#note[
  dcrd signs with the deterministic nonce of its EC-Schnorr-DCRv0
  implementation, which makes the signature vector of @sec:mixing-vectors
  reproducible. Verifiers do not depend on how the nonce was chosen.
]

=== Transcript references <sec:mixing-seen>

Every message after the pair request lists the hashes of the messages of the
previous stage that the sender used: the KE lists the session's PRs
(`SeenPRs`), the CT lists KEs, the SR lists CTs, the FP and DC list SRs, the
CM lists DCs, and an RS lists RSs. Because the lists are inside the signed
data, each message commits to the transcript its author saw. The reference
implementation reads only two of them: the `SeenPRs` of a KE, which define
the session (@alg:mixing-sid, @alg:mixing-altses), and the `SeenSecrets` of
an RS, which are used for blame (@sec:mixing-blame-proc). For the CT, SR,
FP, DC, CM and RS lists the mixpool checks only the number of entries
(@tab:mixing-limits), and the client never inspects the hashes of the CT,
SR, FP, DC and CM lists. The client
fills these lists in unmixed order, except `SeenSecrets`, whose order is
unspecified. An implementation #SHOULD fill them exactly as described in
@sec:mixing-run. #src("mixing/mixpool/limits.go", "checkCTLimits") | #src("mixing/mixclient/blame.go", "blame")

== Wire Messages <sec:mixing-wire>

The eight messages are valid only on connections that negotiated protocol
version #const("MixVersion") $= 10$ or later (#chref("p2p")); decoding them
at a lower version is an error. They are announced and requested with
inventory vectors of type `MSG_MIX` $= 4$ whose hash is the message hash. All
integers are little-endian; "hash" fields are 32 raw bytes. Each table gives
the payload of the message, which follows the common message header.

The messages share a prefix: the 64-byte Schnorr signature, the 33-byte
identity, and (except in the PR) the 32-byte session ID and the 4-byte run
number. Every message produced by the reference client carries run number
0, and the mixpool rejects any other value before all other checks
(@sec:mixing-mixpool-accept). #src("mixing/mixpool/mixpool.go", "AcceptMessage")

